    "priority": ["ichi1", "news1", "nf02"],
    "stratum": "wago",
    "processes": ["derivation", "unclear"],
    "headline": "Mukashi is muka, from the verb 向く (muku, “to face opposite”), plus a direction suffix し (shi): “that which stands opposite from the present”.",
    "morphemes": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "quote": "From Old Japanese. The muka portion derives from the 未然形 (mizenkei, “incomplete form”) of verb 向く (muku, “to face opposite to something”), implying “that which stands opposite from the present”. The shi portion is suffix し (shi) indicating direction, also found in words 縦し (tatashi, “the standing or vertical direction”), 横し (yokoshi, “the sideways or horizontal direction”). This appears to be a somewhat rare alternative form, more commonly encountered as さ (sa), and may be cognate with the nominalizing adjective ending さ (sa)."
      }
    ],
    "examples": [
      {
        "id": 142393,
        "ja": "昔はよくした。",
        "en": "I used to a lot.",
        "enId": 272177,
        "form": "昔",
